  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSurface high pressure dominates the region this Sunday \nafternoon, offering ample sunshine, lights winds, and \ntemperatures in the 60s. These conditions extend through \ntonight, with a slight increase in winds from the south. This \nwill help temperatures from tanking into the 30s, with low \ntemperatures forecast into the 40s. This should also help limit \nthe chance for frost, however, there may be some localized spots\nin northwest MN near Lake of the Woods to Red Lakes area should\nwinds stay calm with the help of denser tree cover.\n\nStill on track for widespread rainfall and gusty winds Monday\nmorning, generally after 8 AM. A stronger upper shortwave \ntrough turning closed low moves through the northern High Plains\ninto south-central Canada on Monday. This will bring a plume of\nmoisture and forcing as it approaches the region to the \nmajority of the Dakotas and Minnesota. This will also bring \ngusty winds both ahead and behind the wave. Minor wind impacts\nare forecast in portions of northeast ND Monday night into\nTuesday where a Wind Advisory is valid.\n\nLooking into mid to late next week, ensemble's evolution of the \nupper pattern continues to show signs of upper ridging, \nindicating at temperature trend that may gradually increase. \nHowever, the lack of more robust upper ridging means we should \nnot see a drastic increased trend in temperatures, likely \nkeeping them closer to average to slightly above average.\n\n...High chance for wetting rainfall...\n\nAll guidance continues to agree in a leading plume of moisture \nand lift overspreading our entire region on Monday, bringing \nwidespread rainfall for majority of the area. Within this plume\nwill also likely be weak instability, offering convective \nelements to percolate within the widespread rainfall. While \nlightning is not expected to be widespread/numerous, there \nshould still be low level of activity/isolated instances of\nlightning. This should bring at least a wetting rainfall to \nmajority of the region generally between 0.2-0.7 inches of\nrainfall.\n\nSome guidance still offers a scenario that brings around 1 \ninch of rainfall. This will likely be more spotty/isolated due \nto convective elements needed, if it occurs in our area at all.\n\n...Gusty winds Monday and Tuesday...\n\nAs the system rapidly strengthens Monday, it will energize\nsoutherly winds aloft as early as Monday morning over eastern ND\ninto west-central and northwest MN. With the help of topography\nand a tightening pressure gradient, south-southeast winds\ngusting 25-40 mph are forecast - highest within the southern Red\nRiver Valley. While not forecast at this time, there is a low\nchance scenario for isolated gusts 40-55 mph during this first\nwave of gusty winds within the Red River Valley. It also remains\nunclear how convective elements within rainfall may help\ntransfer strong winds aloft toward the surface.\n\nAs the day progresses and the system moves into south-central\nCanada, a cold front sweeps west to east across our area Monday\nevening. This will bring a second wave of gusty winds, this time\nout of the west/northwest, and likely to be a bit stronger.\nGusts 30-50 mph are currently forecast, highest in Devils Lake\nbasin of northeast ND. This is where a Wind Advisory is in\neffect Monday evening into Tuesday morning.\n\nThere is a scenario where even higher gusts 50-60 mph become\npresent as early as Monday afternoon with the help of deep\nmixing amid the dry slot of the strong low. This could then be\nfollowed by similar gusts behind the cold front. Should this\noccur, a High Wind Warning may be needed - although confidence\nin this scenario is low. Lowered confidence in this higher\nimpact winds stem from uncertainties in timing of the dry slot\nas well as strength of cold air advection/mixing potential.",
